-- title:
--   Vanishing étale coordinate for Hecke reductions of Gauss-reducing divisors
-- statement:
--   The statement concerns the two-chart model of $X_1(Mp)$ over a discrete valuation ring and its relative Picard scheme, and asserts the vanishing of the $J_E$-coordinate of a reduced Hecke translate.
--
--   **Arithmetic setting.** Fixed are a prime $p$, an integer $M$ with $5 \le M$ and $p \nmid M$, a field $L$ of characteristic zero which is a $\{p\}$-cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$, a primitive $p$-th root of unity $\zeta \in L$, and an intermediate field $K$ of $L \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ with $K =$ [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), the $L$-subfield of $L((q))$ generated by the coefficientwise image of the function field of $X_1(Mp)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ (hypothesis `hK`). Further, $A$ is a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$ such that $p$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$ (`hAp`) and $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A \to L$ (`hζA`), $K$ carries an $A$-algebra structure compatible with that of $L$, and $j \in K$ is a nonzero element whose Laurent series is the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) (`hj`). The curve over $A$ is [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A ↥K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L252), assumed proper.
--
--   **Special fibre and its two components.** Here $k$ is an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$ and an $A$-algebra; $c_1 : C_1 \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ and $c_2 : C_2 \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ are proper, smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral; $i_1, i_2$ are closed immersions of $C_1, C_2$ into the base change `baseChange A (ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A ↥K j) k` over $k$. The hypothesis `hcover` says every point of that base change lies in the image of $i_1$ or of $i_2$; `hred` says the intersection scheme `pullback i₁.1 i₂.1` is reduced, and `hn`, `hn0` say it has exactly $n$ points with $n > 0$.
--
--   **Sections.** $\varepsilon$ is a section of the model over $\operatorname{Spec} A$, and $\varepsilon_1, \varepsilon_2$ are $k$-sections of $c_1, c_2$; `hε₁` requires $\varepsilon_1$ followed by $i_1$ to be the base-changed section `sectionBaseChange k ε`.
--
--   **Relative Picard data.** $D$ is a `RelativePic0Designation` for the model over $A$ (a scheme with a structure morphism to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ and a zero section), and `hrep` asserts that $D$ represents the relative Picard functor of the model, rigidified along $\varepsilon$ and cut out by fibrewise algebraic equivalence to zero; `hsm`, `hsep` require $D$'s structure morphism to be smooth and separated. The hypothesis `hreps` is the corresponding representability statement for `D.baseChange k` over $k$, and `hPk` identifies its Poincaré bundle with the base change along $k$ of the Poincaré bundle of $D$ pulled back along the first projection. Similarly $D_1, D_2$ with `hrep₁`, `hrep₂` represent the relative Picard functors of $c_1, c_2$ rigidified along $\varepsilon_1, \varepsilon_2$. The morphism $\nu_2$ from `(D.baseChange k).toBase` to `D₂.toBase` over $k$ is characterised by `hν₂`: for every base $t : T \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ and every point $a$ of `(D.baseChange k).toBase` over $t$, the pullback of the $D_2$-Poincaré bundle along $a$ followed by $\nu_2$ is isomorphic to the rigidification, along `rigSection c₂ t ε₂` and the projection `pullback.snd c₂ t`, of the pullback along `curveChange i₂.1 i₂.2 t` of the pullback of the $k$-Poincaré bundle along $a$; thus $\nu_2$ is restriction of line bundles to the component $C_2$.
--
--   **Geometric generic fibre.** With compatible algebra maps $A \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ and $L \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (for $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`), $M\eta$ is a `CurveModel` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with function field [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182), and $e\eta$ is an isomorphism from $M\eta.C$ to the base change of the model along $\operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb{Q}} \to \operatorname{Spec} A$, compatible with the structure morphisms (`heη`). The chart hypotheses are: `Mη_chart_nonempty`, that the preimage of the image of the finite chart [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.ιFin A ↥K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L231) under $e\eta$ followed by the first projection is nonempty; and `hMηpin`, that for every element $a$ of the finite-chart algebra [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.chartAlgFin A ↥K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L135) the function-field element obtained by transporting $a$ through this chart identification has Laurent series equal to the coefficientwise image under $L \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the Laurent series of $a$. The hypothesis `hgal` is Galois equivariance: for every $g \in \mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ fixing $L$ elementwise and all $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x, x'$ of $M\eta.C$, if $x'$ composed into the finite chart equals $\operatorname{Spec}(g)$ followed by $x$ composed into the finite chart, then the place attached to $x'$ by `Mη.pointEquivPlace` is the translate of the place attached to $x$ by [`ModularCurve.arithmeticGalois (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p)) g`](def/ModularCurve_ArithmeticGalois.html#L54).
--
--   **Special-fibre group datum.** $G$ is a [`ModularCurve.JOneP.NeronSpecialFibreGeom p`](def/ModularCurve_JOnePGeom.html#L9): abelian groups $J_{0s}, J_I, J_E$, a subgroup `torus` of $J_{0s}$, and a surjective homomorphism $\mathrm{proj} : J_{0s} \to J_I \times J_E$ with kernel `torus`. Bijections `pts`, `ptsI`, `ptsE` identify $J_{0s}, J_I, J_E$ with the $k$-points of `(D.baseChange k).toBase`, `D₁.toBase`, `D₂.toBase`; `hadd`, `haddI`, `haddE` say each bijection turns addition into tensor product of the corresponding pullbacks of the Poincaré bundles; `hproj` says that for every $x \in J_{0s}$ one has $\mathrm{ptsI}((\mathrm{proj}\,x)_1)$ equal to `pts x` followed by the induced map `RepresentsRelSubPic.pullbackHom i₁.1 i₁.2 hε₁ hreps hrep₁.some` on relative $\mathrm{Pic}^0$, and $\mathrm{ptsE}((\mathrm{proj}\,x)_2)$ equal to `pts x` followed by $\nu_2$.
--
--   **Generic dictionary and Hecke operators.** `gpts` is a bijection from [`ModularCurve.JOne (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L186), the group of degree-zero divisor classes of [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182), onto the points of `D.toBase` over $\operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb{Q}} \to \operatorname{Spec} A$, additive for the relative group law attached to `hrep.some` (`hgadd`). The family $\varphi$ assigns to each element $t$ of the polynomial Hecke algebra [`ModularCurve.HeckeAlgOne`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L16) an endomorphism of `D.toBase` over $A$; `hφmul` says each $\varphi(t)$ is additive for the relative group law on every base, and `hφpts` says that for the module structure [`ModularCurve.heckeModuleOneBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L129) one has $\mathrm{gpts}(t \cdot x)$ equal to $\mathrm{gpts}(x)$ followed by $\varphi(t)$.
--
--   **Abel–Jacobi data.** The hypothesis `hDL` is representability after base change to $L$, with Poincaré comparison `hPL`; `ajL` is a morphism over $L$ from the base-changed model to `(D.baseChange L).toBase` sending the base-changed section to the zero section (`hajLε`) and satisfying the Abel–Jacobi property `hajL`: for every field $K'$, every $t : \operatorname{Spec} K' \to \operatorname{Spec} L$ and every point $x$ of the base-changed curve over $t$, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle along $x$ followed by `ajL` is isomorphic to the line bundle of the relative effective Cartier divisor of the point $x$ tensored with the ideal module of the divisor of the section. Further, `kL` is a comparison morphism between the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$- and $L$-base changes of the model, compatible with both projections (`hkL₁`, `hkL₂`); `ajbar` is defined (`hajbar`) as $e\eta$ followed by `kL`, `ajL` and the first projection, and lies over $M\eta$'s structure morphism followed by $\operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb{Q}} \to \operatorname{Spec} A$ (`hajbar_over`); $\bar\varepsilon$ is a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point of $M\eta.C$ lying over $\varepsilon$ in the chart (`hεbar`) and sent by `ajbar` to the zero section (`hεbar_aj`). Finally `hpts_aj` says: for all $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x, s$ of $M\eta.C$ with $s$ composed into the chart equal to the base change of $\varepsilon$, there is a degree-zero divisor $D_v$ whose underlying divisor is $[\,\mathrm{place}(x)\,] - [\,\mathrm{place}(s)\,]$ and with $\mathrm{gpts}$ of its class equal to $x$ followed by `ajbar`.
--
--   **Igusa-curve data.** $w$ is an integral weight-one form of level $M$ over $k$ (a weight-one form for $\Gamma_1(M)$ with an integral $q$-expansion whose reduction over $k$ is nonzero), $\mathrm{Mdl}_1$ a curve model over $k$ with function field [`ModularCurve.igusaFunctionFieldX1C k M w`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaFunctionFieldX1.html#L35), and $e_1$ an isomorphism $\mathrm{Mdl}_1.C \cong C_1$ over $k$ (`he₁`), with the relevant chart preimage nonempty (`hne₁`). The hypothesis `hgauss₁` is the Gauss-reduction compatibility: for every $a$ in the finite-chart algebra and all power series $x, y$ over $A$ with the reduction of $y$ nonzero, if the Laurent series of $a$ times the image of $y$ equals the image of $x$ over $L$, then the corresponding element of the Igusa function field has Laurent series over $k$ equal to the quotient of the reductions of $x$ and $y$. The isomorphism $\theta_1 : J_I \cong \mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the Igusa function field satisfies `hθpin₁`: whenever the Poincaré pullback along $\mathrm{ptsI}(g)$ is isomorphic to the line bundle of a $k$-point $x$ of $c_1$ tensored with the ideal module of $\varepsilon_1$, there is a degree-zero divisor equal to $[\,\mathrm{place}(x)\,] - [\,\mathrm{place}(\varepsilon_1)\,]$ whose class is $\theta_1(g)$. Finally `frobIg` is a semilinear automorphism of the Igusa function field over $k$ raising every Laurent coefficient to the $p$-th power (`hfrobIg`).
--
--   **A group subscheme of the Jacobian model.** Here $\mathcal{A}$ with $a : \mathcal{A} \to \operatorname{Spec} A$ and a morphism $\iota$ over $a$ to `D.toBase` which is a closed immersion (`h𝒜cl`), with $a$ proper (`h𝒜pr`) and smooth (`h𝒜sm`) and with connected fibres over every algebraically closed field (`h𝒜conn`). The hypothesis `h𝒜grp` says the points factoring through $\iota$ are closed under the unit, the multiplication and the inversion of the relative group law of $D$ on every base; `h𝒜gen` says that a class $x \in$ [`ModularCurve.JOne (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L186) lies in [`ModularCurve.normFreePartAt (M * p) p`](def/ModularCurve_X1PrimitiveSpecializationAtP.html#L29), the image of the endomorphism `normFreeEnd` at the chosen representatives at $p$, if and only if $\mathrm{gpts}(x)$ factors through $\iota$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$; and `h𝒜hecke` says that composition with $\varphi(t)$ preserves points factoring through $\iota$, for every $t$ in the Hecke algebra and on every base.
--
--   **Valuation data.** $\mathrm{Pl}$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with `Pl.LiesOverPrime p`, i.e. $p$ is a non-unit of $\mathrm{Pl}$; $\rho : A \to \mathrm{Pl}$ lifts $A \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (`hρ`); $O$ is a subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ contained in $\mathrm{Pl}$ (`hO`) with $\rho_O : A \to O$ lifting $A \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (`hρO`); and $\pi_k : \mathrm{Pl} \to k$ is a surjective ring homomorphism (`hπk`) with $A \to k$ equal to $\rho$ followed by $\pi_k$ (`hAlgk`).
--
--   **Conclusion.** For every element $D_v$ of the degree-zero subgroup of the divisor group of [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182) over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, assume the following Gauss-reduction hypothesis on the support: for every place $Q$ in the support of $D_v$ there exist a point $\xi$ of the two-chart model over $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\rho$ and a $k$-point $d$ of $c_1$ such that (i) $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the inclusion $\mathrm{Pl} \subseteq \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ followed by $\xi$ equals the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point `Mη.pointEquivPlace.symm Q` followed by $e\eta$ and the first projection; (ii) $d$ followed by $i_1$ and the first projection equals $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\pi_k$ followed by $\xi$, so that $d$ is the reduction of $\xi$, placed on the component $C_1$; (iii) no point of the source of $d$ is mapped by $d$ into the image of the projection `pullback.fst i₁.1 i₂.1`, that is $d$ avoids the crossing locus of the two components; and (iv) every point of the source is mapped, by $d$ followed by $i_1$ and the first projection, into the image of the finite chart [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.ιFin A ↥K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L231).
--
--   Then for every point $zz$ of `D.toBase` over $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\rho$ such that $\mathrm{gpts}$ of the class of $D_v$ equals $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the inclusion $\mathrm{Pl} \subseteq \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ followed by $zz$, and for every $t' \in J_{0s}$ such that $\mathrm{pts}(t')$ followed by the projection `pullback.fst D.toBase (specMap A k)` equals $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\pi_k$ followed by $zz$ and then by $\varphi$ of the Hecke generator [`ModularCurve.heckeGenOne`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L18) at $p$, one has
--   $$(\mathrm{proj}\,t')_2 = 0 \quad \text{in } J_E.$$
--
--   This is the étale-component half of the Eichler–Shimura reading of the Hecke operator at $p$ on the special fibre of the Jacobian of $X_1(Mp)$: a degree-zero class whose support reduces into the multiplicative (cusp) component away from the crossings, and inside the finite $j$-chart, has Hecke translate at $p$ whose reduction has trivial coordinate in the Jacobian $J_E$ of the étale component. It is used in the subsequent step of the level-lowering argument, where the vanishing of the étale coordinate is propagated from Gauss-reducing divisors to general classes, alongside the companion statement for the cusp component.
